  "output": "## Codebase snapshot\n\nThis is an early-stage Next.js 16 app (Canon) — a landing page, a `get-started` page, and exactly **one** data-backed console page (`/sources`). There's no client-side data fetching, no `useState`/`useEffect`/`useMemo` anywhere, and only one DB query in the entire codebase (`lib/server/sources.ts:19`). So most of the classic categories you asked about don't have live instances yet — I'm not going to invent bugs that aren't there. Below is what's actually present, plus forward-looking guidance tied to code comments that explicitly flag what's coming next (run-status polling via TanStack Query), since that's exactly where these problems will appear first.\n\n### 1. N+1 queries — none present, but one redundant-refetch pattern\n\n`countSourcesBySide()` calls `listSources()`, which re-runs the full `select()` **and** re-validates every row through Zod, just to produce three counts:\n\n```ts\n// lib/server/sources.ts:23\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources();   // full query + full-table Zod parse\n  return { crm: all.filter(...).length, warehouse: ..., synthetic: ... };\n}\n```\n\nIf any page ever calls both `listSources()` and `countSourcesBySide()` (likely, once a dashboard summarizes sources), that's two round trips + double parsing for data you already have. Push the count into SQL instead:\n\n```ts\nimport { sql } from \"drizzle-orm\";\n\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const [row] = await getDb()\n    .select({\n      crm: sql<number>`count(*) filter (where ${sources.kind} in ('salesforce','hubspot'))`,\n      warehouse: sql<number>`count(*) filter (where ${sources.kind} in ('databricks','snowflake'))`,\n      synthetic: sql<number>`count(*) filter (where ${sources.kind} = 'synthetic')`,\n    })\n    .from(sources);\n  return row;\n}\n```\n\n### 2. React re-renders — no state to over-render, but client-boundary bloat\n\nNothing memoizes/re-renders unnecessarily today because nothing holds state. What *is* worth fixing: seven files are marked `\"use client\"` purely to call `useReducedMotion()`/`motion.div` (`landing-hero.tsx`, `pipeline-strip.tsx`, `get-started-hero.tsx`, `step-list.tsx`, `sources-empty-state.tsx`, plus `nav-rail.tsx` and `button.tsx` for unrelated reasons). Each one ships `motion/react` into the client bundle for an otherwise-static landing page. Since these are Server Components by default, the fix is to keep the copy/markup server-rendered and isolate only the animated wrapper as a small client leaf:\n\n```tsx\n// components/motion-fade.tsx  (\"use client\" — the only client boundary)\n\"use client\";\nexport function MotionFade({ children, delay = 0 }: { children: React.ReactNode; delay?: number }) {\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  return (\n    <motion.div initial={{ opacity: 0, y: reduced ? 0 : 6 }} animate={{ opacity: 1, y: 0 }}\n      transition={{ duration: 0.12, delay: reduced ? 0 : delay, ease: [0.2, 0, 0.2, 1] }}>\n      {children}\n    </motion.div>\n  );\n}\n```\n`LandingHero`, `PipelineStrip`, etc. become Server Components again, wrapping their static JSX in `<MotionFade>`. Less client JS, same visual result.\n\n**Forward-looking:** `lib/server/sources.ts:9` explicitly notes \"TanStack Query is reserved for run-status polling only\" — when `/runs` is built, poll at the smallest component (a status badge), not the page, and gate the query with `enabled: isRunActive(status)` (already exported from `types/run.ts:81`) so completed runs stop re-rendering on every interval tick.\n\n### 3. Caching opportunities\n\n`listSources()` calls `connection()` (`lib/server/sources.ts:17`), which opts the whole `/sources` route out of static rendering — every request is a live DB round trip, even though `sources` is low-churn config data. Since writes are the only thing that changes it, wrap it in `unstable_cache` keyed by a tag and revalidate on write instead of hitting Postgres every request:\n\n```ts\nimport { unstable_cache } from \"next/cache\";\n\nexport const listSources = unstable_cache(\n  async (): Promise<Source[]> => {\n    const rows = await getDb().select().from(sources).orderBy(sources.createdAt);\n    return rows.map((row) => sourceSchema.parse(row));\n  },\n  [\"sources:list\"],\n  { tags: [\"sources\"] },\n);\n```\nThen call `revalidateTag(\"sources\")` wherever a source is created/deleted. (One good pattern already in place: `getDb()` caches the Postgres client on `globalThis` to survive HMR reloads — keep that.)\n\n### 4. Memory leaks — none found\n\nNo `setInterval`/`setTimeout`/event listeners/subscriptions anywhere, so there's nothing to clean up yet. The one thing to watch: when polling lands, make sure the effect's cleanup actually clears the interval/EventSource and that polling stops on unmount *and* on terminal status — easy to leak intervals if the stop condition only checks unmount.\n\n### 5. Redundant computation\n\n`listSources()` runs `sourceSchema.parse(row)` on **every row, every request** (`lib/server/sources.ts:20`), even though Drizzle's schema (`lib/db/schema.ts:42`) already enforces the shape at the column-type level and this data isn't external input. Zod validation earns its cost at the boundary where you *write* untrusted input (`createSourceSchema`, already used correctly for that purpose) — re-validating trusted rows read back from your own DB on every page load is pure overhead that scales linearly with source count for no correctness benefit:\n\n```ts\nexport async function listSources(): Promise<Source[]> {\n  await connection();\n  return getDb().select().from(sources).orderBy(sources.createdAt); // trust the Drizzle types\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n**Bottom line:** there's nothing to fix urgently — the codebase is too small to have accumulated real perf debt. The two changes worth making now (SQL-side counting, dropping the redundant re-parse) are cheap and prevent bad habits from calcifying before `/runs`, `/conflicts`, and `/rules` get built out with actual query volume and polling.\n",
